# PHASE 6 — REAL CONCURRENT USER & LOAD TESTING

This is one of the highest-priority phases.

I want to understand how many concurrent users the website can actually support and where it begins to slow down.

Do not use vague claims like "supports unlimited users."

Create a realistic load-testing strategy.

## A. Test environment

Use:

- Isolated staging deployment
- Test database
- Synthetic users
- Synthetic data
- Separate credentials
- Controlled test traffic

Do not send uncontrolled load to the live production website.

Do not test against third-party APIs at high volume without authorization.

## B. User scenarios

Create realistic virtual-user journeys:

### Scenario 1 — Homepage visitor

- Open homepage
- Load images and assets
- Navigate sections
- Open login page

### Scenario 2 — Authenticated dashboard user

- Login
- Open dashboard
- Load incidents
- Load risk overview
- View map
- View resource status

### Scenario 3 — Emergency operator

- Open incident list
- Filter incidents
- Open incident detail
- Update incident
- Create incident report
- View risk assessment

### Scenario 4 — Logistics user

- Open inventory
- View warehouse stock
- Search resources
- View relief requests
- Generate allocation proposal

### Scenario 5 — AI user

- Start AI coordination session
- Retrieve agent results
- View plan
- Validate recommendations

AI calls must be controlled so load tests do not generate uncontrolled AI costs.

### Scenario 6 — Realtime users

- Connect
- Subscribe
- Receive test events
- Disconnect
- Reconnect

## C. Gradual load test

Start conservatively.

Suggested test stages:

| Stage | Concurrent virtual users | Duration |
|---|---:|---:|
| Baseline | 1–5 | 2 min |
| Low load | 10 | 5 min |
| Normal load | 25 | 5 min |
| Medium load | 50 | 10 min |
| High load | 100 | 10 min |
| Stress test | 200 | Controlled |
| Capacity test | Increase gradually | Stop at failure threshold |

Only perform the stages that the environment and resource limits safely permit.

These user counts are test levels, not assumptions that the current server can handle them.

Do not jump directly to 1,000 users.

Do not execute the entire stress test against production.

## D. Measure at each stage

Record:

- Concurrent users
- Requests per second
- Successful requests
- Failed requests
- HTTP status codes
- p50 latency
- p90 latency
- p95 latency
- p99 latency
- Average response time
- Timeouts
- CPU
- Memory
- Database connections
- Database latency
- Queue depth
- Realtime connection count
- External API latency
- Server restart count

Create a performance report.

Identify the first bottleneck.

## E. Failure thresholds

Define safe stop conditions before testing.

Stop or reduce load if:

- Error rate exceeds 5% for sustained periods.
- p95 latency exceeds the defined critical threshold.
- CPU remains saturated.
- Memory approaches the safe limit.
- Database connections are exhausted.
- Request queue grows continuously.
- Server restarts unexpectedly.
- Database begins failing.
- External service limits are approached.
- Test traffic impacts other users.

Do not continue stressing a system after it has begun destabilizing.

## F. Capacity determination

Estimate the sustainable concurrent user capacity based on actual results.

Report:

- Stable concurrency
- Peak tested concurrency
- Concurrency at which performance degraded
- Bottleneck
- Recommended production capacity
- Safety margin
- Scaling recommendation

Do not extrapolate a tested capacity into an unsupported unlimited-user claim.

