# PHASE 7 — SERVER RESILIENCE & OVERLOAD PROTECTION

Make the server resilient to sudden traffic spikes.

Inspect the server architecture and determine whether it is capable of handling concurrent requests efficiently.

Implement only appropriate safeguards.

## Protection mechanisms

- Request timeouts
- Connection limits
- Concurrency limits
- Rate limiting
- Request queue management
- Graceful overload responses
- Memory protection
- CPU-intensive work isolation
- Background processing for long tasks
- Safe caching
- Circuit breakers for failing external services
- Retry limits
- Graceful shutdown
- Health checks

## Overload behavior

When the server becomes overloaded:

- Do not crash immediately.
- Do not allow memory to grow without control.
- Do not create unlimited pending tasks.
- Do not overwhelm the database.
- Do not retry every failed request at once.
- Do not queue unlimited AI tasks.
- Return appropriate retryable responses where necessary.
- Preserve critical operations.
- Reject nonessential work safely when necessary.

Use appropriate HTTP responses, such as 429 or 503, when warranted.

Emergency incident intake and essential operations should be prioritized over expensive nonessential analytics or AI workloads where the architecture permits.

